# Build-context exclusions. Everything here is either reproduced inside the image by a
# verified step, or must never enter an image at all.

**/.git

# config/local.yaml is the gitignored developer override, and on a real checkout it holds
# the >=32-character Yar session-seal password that app.js refuses to start without. The
# build context is not the repository: `COPY . …` copies whatever is on disk, so without
# this line every image built from a developer's working tree baked that developer's
# session secret into a shipped layer, readable by anyone who could pull the image. Nothing
# in the container needs it - the deployed configuration comes from NODE_ENV and the
# environment - so it is excluded outright rather than managed.
config/local.yaml

# The rest of node-config's gitignored file set, excluded for exactly the same reason as
# config/local.yaml above. Naming one variant was not enough: `.gitignore` lines 16-28 also
# ignore config/runtime.json and its .tmp-* siblings, config/development.{yaml,yml,json},
# config/production.{yaml,yml,json}, config/local.{yml,json} and config/migration{,Db}.json.
# Every one of those is a place a real deployment secret lives - config/production.yaml is
# the documented deployment path - and because none of them is tracked, `git status` on the
# build host stays clean while `COPY . …` copies them verbatim into a layer. An operator who
# builds on a host holding a real config/production.yaml would otherwise bake production
# credentials into the image and get no signal that they had.
#
# Wildcards rather than exact names, so a new variant of an already-secret-bearing file is
# excluded by default instead of needing this list to be remembered. The deployed
# configuration reaches the container through NODE_ENV and the environment; nothing in the
# image reads any of these paths.
config/local.*
config/production.*
config/development.*
config/runtime.json*
config/migration*.json

# The two TRACKED templates the wildcards above would otherwise have swept up. Both are in
# git, both are secret-free by construction, and both were in every image built before this
# exclusion list existed, so removing them would be an unrelated change to the image's
# contents rather than the hardening this list is for. `.dockerignore` applies the last
# matching pattern, so these two re-include exactly themselves and nothing else.
!config/local.example.yaml
!config/production.yaml.dist

# node_modules is produced inside the builder by `npm ci` from the committed lockfile.
# Shipping the host's copy into the context (210MB here) both slows every build and lets
# host state influence a step whose entire purpose is to be determined by the lockfile
# alone.
node_modules

# public/components is the 435MB vendored component tree. The builder downloads it from the
# tagged release and checks it against a published sha256 before extracting; letting an
# unverified host copy into the context would put bytes into the image that never passed
# that gate, and would silently defeat the check for anyone who already had the tree.
public/components
